{"acceptance_length": 2.1333333333333333, "acceptance_lengths": [4, 5, 1, 2, 2, 1, 2, 3, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, 6, 3, 2, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, 3, 2, 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 4, 1, 3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 1, 4, 2, 1, 2, 1, 1, 7, 4, 4, 1, 1, 5, 10, 3, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 2, 5, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 1, 1, 3, 2, 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 1, 5, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 5, 3, 4], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 20538876928, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 20538874368, "benchmark": "mtbench", "completion": "The **base rate fallacy** is a cognitive bias in which people ignore **base rates** (the general probability of an event occurring in the population) when making decisions or judgments. Instead, they focus on **specific, but possibly irrelevant**, information. This leads to incorrect conclusions because the base rate is often more important in determining the likelihood of an outcome.\n\nFor example, if a disease has a 1% prevalence rate (base rate) in the population and a test for the disease is 99% accurate, the probability that someone who tests positive actually has the disease is not 99%, but much lower due to the low base rate. This is a classic example of the base rate fallacy.\n\n---\n\n### **Five Examples of How Politicians Use the Base Rate Fallacy in Campaigns**\n\n1. **Misrepresenting Crime Rates**  \n   A politician might claim, \"Crime is rising in our city!\" but fail to mention that the **base rate** of crime in the city is still lower than the national average. By focusing on a few high-profile crimes (which are more memorable), they create a misleading impression of an increasing trend.\n\n2. **Using Anecdotal Evidence to Support Policies**  \n   A candidate might say, \"I\u2019ve", "decode_seconds": 4.310905288904905, "draft_calls": 120, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 33, "method": "native_target_dflash", "output_hash": "ba4d1ab5bf10fdd77d818559c593ac8936e1e24d33e8a6c2de44fa492fc2e876", "ou